<h2>4. Infrastructure Layer (Terraform)</h2>

<p>Terraform provisions the <strong>shared, environment-agnostic</strong> infrastructure:</p>

<div class="card">
<h3>Resources Created</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Resource</th><th>Description</th></tr>
<tr><td>VPC</td><td>Custom VPC with public/private subnets across 2 AZs</td></tr>
<tr><td>EKS Cluster</td><td>Single Kubernetes cluster (v1.30) shared by all environments</td></tr>
<tr><td>EKS Node Group</td><td>Managed node group (t3.medium) for workloads</td></tr>
<tr><td>EC2 (Nginx)</td><td>Bastion/proxy instance for kubeconfig and Datadog</td></tr>
<tr><td>ECR Repos</td><td>5 Elastic Container Repositories for app images</td></tr>
<tr><td>Security Groups</td><td>Cluster, node, and Nginx instance security groups</td></tr>
<tr><td>IAM Roles</td><td>EKS cluster role, node role, ALB controller role</td></tr>
<tr><td>S3 Backend</td><td>Remote Terraform state in S3 + DynamoDB locking</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<div class="note">
  Terraform is <strong>NOT</strong> run per environment. It's run once via the manual <code>infra.yml</code> workflow to create shared infrastructure. Environments are then deployed as separate namespaces via Helm.
</div>

